# config/risk.yaml IS tracked, deliberately. It was ignored at first, on
# the reasoning that an owner's risk numbers are theirs and not the
# repository's. That turned out to be wrong twice over: the file is the
# single most consequential thing in the deployment, so a change to it
# deserves the same history as a change to the engine; and an untracked
# file does not survive a fresh clone, which left the platform refusing
# to start with no way for the owner to fix it. It holds no credentials.
# Recorded market data can be large and is often licensed.
data/recordings/*.csv
